  "verbalization": "Trandolapril, Moexipril, and Cilazapril are all members of a class of compounds that share structural similarities and are classified as Ace Inhibitors or Dicarboxylic Acid Monoesters. These relationships are supported by multiple paths in the hetionet dataset, which indicate that these drugs resemble one another and bind to Slc15a2, a gene involved in ion transport. The common ancestors of these drugs—Ace Inhibitors and Dicarboxylic Acid Monoesters—suggest a shared biological function, potentially involving the regulation of ion homeostasis. The binding of these drugs to Slc15a2 implies a possible mechanism of action related to ion transport or cellular signaling, though the exact functional role of Slc15a2 in this context remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence from the dataset supports a mechanistic link between Trandolapril and Slc15a2, but further experimental validation is needed to confirm the specific interaction and its biological significance.",
